s  together and it's really collaborative  process and from what I've read they did  the same thing here with all the main  cast where they would meet up you know  discuss the script they would tailor the  script to things going on in the actors  lives especially I would imagine our  Coltrane who plays Mason who is the main  character I mean this movie gives you  insight and focuses on a lot of  different characters throughout but  Mason is sort of your main drive for the  story so all the actors weighed in on  the script and everything was sort of  subject to changing and morphing just  around life and you know they would  check in every couple years to continue  shooting and it's really amazing that  all these people stuck around and saw  this thing through which right there I  got to applaud everybody involved  because that's you know that's a special  thing especially because they couldn't  sign contracts because there are laws in  place you know prohibiting you from from  contracting people for such a long time  but this movie is pretty incredible to  watch you know you see these actors you  know growing and changing over time you  know for real you know you you see  Patricia Arquette and Ethan Hawke as  well as Lorelei Linklater who plays  their daughter the sister of Mason who's  the main character and Mason is played  by a young man by the name of LR  Coltrane you could see these people i'll  change for the younger actors you get to  see them grow up you get to see their  you know acting shift and grow it's  really interesting just in format alone  and then when you scratch the surface of  the the content of the story it's even  better I mean this story like i said is  a coming-of-age story and it's just sort  of about life it's one of those life  movies one of the things this movie does  such a good job at is just capturing  that sort of vastness of life you know  that the grayness that comes  you know day to day living and just  trying to figure out how to